<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Sensitivity.</span><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
At a fixed budget, the length-bias knob </span><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in </span><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\mathrm{price}/\mathrm{cost}^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">price</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">cost</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\mathrm{price}/\mathrm{cost}^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> smoothly trades off many-short vs. fewer-long prompts;</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AGNews <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2015</a>)</cite></h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We cast each item as an instruction–label pair and evaluate at fixed kept rates. As shown in Table </span><a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ AGNews ( , ) ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Market-Based Data Subset Selection - Principled Aggregation of Multi-Criteria Example Utility" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, </span><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Market+Balanced</em><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> attains the best accuracy at small budgets (kept=5: 0.921; kept=10: 0.931), while at a moderate budget (kept=25) both market variants </span><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">tie for best</em><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> at 0.940. Across budgets, both market formulations match or exceed the single-metric baselines (e.g., Loss-only at 0.933 for kept=25), whereas classical diversity/uncertainty heuristics remain below the market results, and geometry-only baselines underperform markedly (Forgetting-probe and KCenter-Greedy). At kept=25, </span><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Market+Balanced</em><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> achieves the same top accuracy as </span><em id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Market</em><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (0.940) while enforcing perfect balance (see the “Balanced” column), indicating that light balancing preserves top-line accuracy while improving coverage and stability. Taken together with GSM8K, these results indicate that the market formulation delivers competitive accuracy at fixed budget, and that balancing can be applied with negligible loss—often none—at moderate budgets.</span></p>
</div>
