<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Limitations.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our market formulation aggregates heterogeneous signals with transparent knobs, but several caveats remain. At aggressive budgets when the token budget admits very few items the selector can over-weight hard or verbose prompts, slightly worsening early validation loss; balanced topic floors, a mild curriculum, and the length-bias knob </span><math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> mitigate this tendency. Fairness across topics is not automatic, although the topic-separable LMSR avoids dominance, small or under-represented topics may still be under-sampled if their signals are noisy. Another limitation is robustness to misspecified or adversarial signals, if one signal is poorly calibrated, its contribution
may distort prices, though clipping and within-topic standardization reduce this risk. Finally, while the selector is lightweight, the diversity head based on kNN can be compute-heavy at scale, so we employ approximate NN search and two-stage filtering. A further open question is transferability, while we tune on GSM8K and AGNews, settings may need re-calibration in multi-modal or cross-lingual corpora where tokenization, topic balance, and signal
distributions differ substantially.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Data subset selection.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Prior work spans (i) geometric coverage via coresets and submodular objectives from </span><math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-center and
facility-location to data-efficient coresets </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sener &amp; Savarese, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirzasoleiman et al., 2020</a><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, (ii)
gradient- or bilevel-based selection that aligns a small subset with the full training/validation
objective </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirzasoleiman et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Killamsetty et al., 2021</a><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, and (iii) uncertainty/diversity
criteria from active learning such as entropy, margin, BALD, and hybrid diversity samplers
</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Settles, 2009a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ash et al., 2020</a><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Beyond classic heuristics, training-dynamics scores (e.g.,
forgetting events, EL2N, GraNd) identify influential or difficult points early in training and have
been used for pruning and curation </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toneva et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paul et al., 2021</a><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Most methods optimize
a </span><em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.19" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">kept rate</em><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or cardinality </span><math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">; comparatively fewer account explicitly for </span><em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.22" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">token/compute
budgets</em><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, where items have different lengths and the decision should be price-</span><em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.24" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">per-token</em><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> rather
than price-per-item.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Combining multiple signals.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In practice, practitioners often combine signals ad hoc (weighted sums, cascades); principled mixtures
include submodular combinations and bilevel objectives tuned to validation loss
</span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mirzasoleiman et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Killamsetty et al., 2021</a><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. Our approach instead casts multi-signal
aggregation as </span><em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">pricing</em><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> in a cost-function prediction market (LMSR): signals act as traders, a
single liquidity/temperature </span><math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> controls how sharply prices concentrate, and topic-wise
normalization stabilizes cross-topic calibration. This yields a convex, probabilistic combination with
interpretable knobs </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hanson, 2007</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lambert et al., 2008</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen &amp; Vaughan, 2010</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abernethy et al., 2013</a><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
We then make compute explicit via a token-aware decision rule </span><math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=p/\ell^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=p/\ell^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> (length bias
</span><math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and optional topic budgets/floors. Prior market-based work in ML typically aggregates model
</span><em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">predictions</em><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> or studies mechanism design; to our knowledge, using LMSR to </span><em id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">price data
examples (or context chunks)</em><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> for budgeted selection is novel relative to subset-selection, coreset,
and training-dynamics lines of work.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our position.</span><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Relative to (i)–(iii), we contribute a compute-aware selector that (a) unifies uncertainty, rarity, and diversity through LMSR pricing with a single liquidity parameter, (b) respects variable token costs via </span><math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-controlled price-per-token, and (c) operates at prompt level for reasoning tasks. Empirically, under fixed token budgets on GSM8K, the market selector attains robust parity with strong single-signal baselines while offering lower dispersion across seeds and interpretable controls over subset shape.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Reproducibility and implementation details</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Data and setup</span><span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
</span><span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GSM8K (reasoning).</span><span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> We use the main split and format each item as </span><span id="S6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">‘‘Question: … Answer:’’</span><span id="S6.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
The training budget is a fixed 60k tokens over selected items (prompt</span><math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">gold answer under the model tokenizer).
All selectors fine-tune the </span><em id="S6.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">same</em><span id="S6.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> backbone with identical optimizer, learning rate, batch size, and steps;
the only difference is the selected subset. Unless noted, we report mean</span><math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">std over seeds
(</span><em id="S6.p1.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GSM8K Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Setup. ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Market-Based Data Subset Selection - Principled Aggregation of Multi-Criteria Example Utility" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> - 3 seeds</em><span id="S6.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">).</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S6.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AGNews (classification).</span><span id="S6.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Each example is cast as an instruction–label pair. We evaluate at fixed kept-rate
budgets (</span><span id="S6.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">kept</span><span id="S6.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">={5,10,25}%) with identical fine-tuning hyperparameters across selectors and report accuracy
(mean</span><math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">std). The </span><em id="S6.p1.1.18" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Market+Balanced</em><span id="S6.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> variant enforces label/topic floors before filling remaining slots by global price.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Signals, pricing, and selection.</span><span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
Signals include token-level NLL (uncertainty) and embedding-space rarity; </span><em id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Market+Diverse</em><span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> adds a lightweight
diversity head. Signals are standardized within topic and combined via a topic-separable LMSR with global liquidity </span><math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.
For </span><span id="S6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">GSM8K</span><span id="S6.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> we convert prices to a token-budgeted decision using </span><math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\mathrm{price}/\mathrm{cost}^{\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">price</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><msup><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">cost</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\mathrm{price}/\mathrm{cost}^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">
(single </span><math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> unless noted). For </span><span id="S6.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">AGNews</span><span id="S6.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> we use a cardinality head: balanced top-</span><math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> with minimal per-topic
(label/topic) floors, or unbalanced top-</span><math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> by price (no token costs, so </span><math id="S6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is unused). We also report a simple balance
score </span><math id="S6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tfrac{1}{2}\|\hat{\pi}-\alpha\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000"><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">π</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">^</mo></mover><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{1}{2}\|\hat{\pi}-\alpha\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to summarize coverage when floors are active. Selection is greedy by the chosen head.
Selection overhead (signals+pricing) is </span><math id="S6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;\!0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;\!0.1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> GPU-hr per run; we log processed tokens and wallclock. Code uses standard
</span><span id="S6.p2.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">transformers</span><span id="S6.p2.1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">/</span><span id="S6.p2.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">datasets</span><span id="S6.p2.1.20" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> and </span><span id="S6.p2.1.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">sentence-transformers</span><span id="S6.p2.1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">; seeds are fixed for data order and initialization </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S6.p2.1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(</span><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wolf et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lhoest et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers &amp; Gurevych, 2019</a><span id="S6.p2.1.25" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">)</span></cite><span id="S6.p2.1.26" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. For ANN we use FAISS/HNSW backends. Code will be made available upon acceptance.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Practical calibration recipe.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We expose three knobs and tune them with a short, deterministic sweep on a tiny dev split,
(1) </span><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Liquidity <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Binary-search </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to hit a target price entropy </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">p</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> band
(e.g., </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\log N-\delta,\log N]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">−</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">δ</mi></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\log N-\delta,\log N]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) or a desired nESS (smoother vs. sharper markets).
(2) </span><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Signal weights <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Start with </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}=1/K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">w</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">k</mi></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}=1/K</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">; optionally apply one pass of Exponentiated-Gradient
over a small grid to match a dev loss proxy.
(3) </span><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Length bias <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Used only for token budgets (GSM8K). Choose </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in\{1.4,1.6,1.8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∈</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.4</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.6</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.8</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in\{1.4,1.6,1.8\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> based on the
compute profile: larger </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> favors many short items; smaller </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> favors fewer long items. We keep </span><math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma{=}1.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">γ</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma{=}1.6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> by default.
All other hyperparameters (optimizer, steps, batch) are matched across selectors.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We introduced a market-based framework for data subset selection that addresses the fundamental challenge of principled multi-signal aggregation. By treating examples as tradeable contracts and using LMSR to aggregate heterogeneous signals, we achieve theoretical guarantees through maximum entropy characterization while maintaining computational efficiency. Experimental validation demonstrates consistent improvements over existing approaches across diverse tasks. The framework shows particular strength for instruction and prompt data curation, where individual
example utility is well-defined and multi-signal aggregation is critical for efficient LLM fine-tuning.
Future work will explore learned signal design, integration with gradient-based or bilevel selectors,
and dynamic curricula that adapt liquidity or length-bias over training epochs. Another promising
direction is scaling hierarchical markets to retrieval-augmented or multi-modal settings, where
structured budgets (per source or modality) are essential. Finally, deploying market-based selection
at web-scale raises both engineering challenges and opportunities for principled governance of data
pipelines. In addition, the market lens opens avenues for connecting data selection with economic-style
mechanism design: liquidity can be tied to confidence intervals, and topic budgets </span><math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">α</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> can
be learned as dynamic endowments. Such connections may yield adaptive selectors that self-adjust
as training progresses.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
